# Intro
A periodic box of uniform gas (1e3 atom/cm^3, 0.1 Msun, 32768 particles, solar
metallicity) with one star of 29.7 Msun at its centre. The example tests that
the light field does not depend on the velocity of the box. Gas and star share a
bulk velocity along x, so a boosted run is the rest run seen from a moving
frame. `velocity` sets the boost in km/s. `ic` selects the gas layout:

- `lattice`: the particles sit on a perfect cubic lattice with a uniform smoothing
  length. The gas moves as a rigid block. This is the clean test.
- `glass`: the particles sit on a glass. The tiny residual forces of the glass
  move the particles differently in the two frames at float32 rounding level.
  The light field then differs by more than the lattice shows.

`compareBoost.py` computes
`D = max |u_boost - u_rest| / max u_rest` over the gas particles, with `u` the
packet specific energy (`PacketSpecificEnergies`, both bands) matched by particle
id. It also prints the largest displacement between the two runs after
the bulk motion is removed. Two identical rest runs give the repeat floor of `D`:
the multithreaded transport changes the last bits from run to run, and `D`
moves in steps of about 6e-8.

`params.yml` runs 32 steps of 1.5625e-6 (internal units) to t = 5e-5, with
cooling off (`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`) so that the maps
show the light only. `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` keeps its default
(`cap`, 0.99 c).

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --enable-isrf-debugging-checks --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. `--enable-isrf-debugging-checks`
adds the `Packet*` snapshot fields that `compareBoost.py` reads. The number of
reception slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). No download is provided. Pass
the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`; the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
ic=lattice velocity=0 ./run.sh
ic=lattice velocity=0 run_dir=run_lattice_v0_repeat ./run.sh
ic=lattice velocity=300 ./run.sh
ic=lattice velocity=1000 ./run.sh
ic=glass velocity=0 ./run.sh
ic=glass velocity=300 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_<ic>_v<velocity>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `ic`, `velocity` (km/s), `gas_density` (atom/cm^3),
`gas_mass` (Msun), `star_mass` (Msun), `swift_exe`, `run_dir` and `extra_args`
(further swift arguments).

# Compare
```
python3 compareBoost.py run_lattice_v0 run_lattice_v300
python3 compareBoost.py run_lattice_v0 run_lattice_v0_repeat
python3 compareBoost.py run_glass_v0 run_glass_v300
```
The first argument is the rest run. The script prints `D` and the displacement
for each snapshot after the first. `--bar` makes it exit with status 1 when `D` of
the last snapshot exceeds the given value.
